<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PermissionSet xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        This comment sits INSIDE the root element deliberately - a comment above the root breaks
        `sf` at source conversion with "unable to find matching parent xml file".
        Broker_Protection_Access / DPEG_Admin_Access are precedents for this pattern
        (Disposition_Deal_Driver was too, until it was retired 2026-08-19 - see the retraction
        below).
        Added 2026-08-10 by the permission set cleanup (KEEP item K4): this set already carried the
        full rationale in its disposition TWIN and only a one-line description here, which made the
        acquisitions half look like the trivial one-field set it is safe to fold away. It is not.

        🔴 DO NOT FOLD THIS SET INTO DPEG_Acquisition_Edit, AND DO NOT DELETE IT AS "TRIVIAL".
        This is a LAYER-5 AUTHORIZATION set: it carries the Acquisition_Deal_Actions custom
        permission - the live authorization grant, read via FeatureManagement.checkPermission.
        The retired User.Deal_Driver__c fieldPermissions entry that used to sit alongside it as
        a rollback lever was REMOVED 2026-08-12 (Phase 2 Step 1 of retiring the driver-flag
        concept - see the PHASE 2 note below). It was the twin of Disposition_Deal_Driver, whose
        own comment argued the same case for the sell side.

        🔴 RETRACTED IN PART, 2026-08-19, EXPLICIT USER DECISION - DISPOSITION ONLY, NOT
        ACQUISITION. Disposition_Deal_Driver was retired at the user's request. The
        Disposition_Deal_Actions custom permission it used to grant did not go with it: it now
        rides permissionsets/DPEG_Disposition_Edit.permissionset-meta.xml, a module DATA set
        assigned via DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG membership, not a dedicated, directly-assigned
        layer-5 authorization set. Disposition is now an explicit, user-chosen EXCEPTION to the
        "driver sets are direct-assigned, never put in a group" pattern this file argues for -
        see DPEG_Disposition_Edit's and permissionsetgroups/DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG
        .permissionsetgroup-meta.xml's own headers for the full rationale and consequence.

        THIS FILE'S OWN REASONING IS UNCHANGED AND STILL GOVERNS ACQUISITION. This set is still
        assigned DIRECTLY rather than through DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG, deliberately, because its
        population is narrower than any group's - that argument does not weaken because
        disposition chose differently. Do not fold this set into a group, and do not treat the
        disposition exception as precedent for doing so here.

        THE GATE IS TWO-FACTOR (ARCHITECTURE.md section 2, "Opportunity deal-action gate is
        TWO-FACTOR"). OpportunityActionPermissionService.hasDealActionAccess() requires BOTH:
          (a) CAPABILITY    - can the user reach the code at all? Apex class access, granted by
                              Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access, NOT this set.
          (b) AUTHORIZATION - does the user hold the Acquisition_Deal_Actions custom permission?
                              Granted ONLY by assignment of THIS set.
                              FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Acquisition_Deal_Actions') simply
                              returns false for a user who lacks the grant - unlike the retired
                              User.Deal_Driver__c FLS read, there is no throw/degrade distinction
                              to reason about here.
        The six deployed Dynamic Actions visibility rules on Opportunity_Record_Page ask the
        identical question ({!$Permission.CustomPermission.Acquisition_Deal_Actions} EQUAL true),
        so the button chrome and the server-side assertion agree by construction.

        THREE WAYS TO BREAK IT, ALL OF WHICH LOOK LIKE TIDYING:
          1. Moving this customPermissions grant into DPEG_Acquisition_Edit (or into
             Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access, the layer-4 capability set) hands factor (b) to
             that set's ENTIRE population as a side effect of editing acquisitions capability.
             The gate becomes one-factor for everyone who holds the broader set.
          2. Replacing the gate with a permission-set MEMBERSHIP check does the same thing in
             code: a user who holds the CAPABILITY set (Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access - can
             reach the Apex) but does NOT hold THIS set - and so lacks the Acquisition_Deal_Actions
             custom permission - is DENIED today (checkPermission returns false) and would be
             GRANTED by a membership-only check that stops asking after factor (a).
             OpportunityActionPermissionServiceTest.capabilitySetWithoutTheAuthorizationSet_isStillDenied
             (renamed from ...hasDealActionAccess_membershipWithoutTheFlag_isStillDenied when the
             mechanism moved off the FLS flag) exists to go red if anyone tries. Do not modify
             that test.
          3. Deleting this set as "trivial" denies EVERY deal driver - silently. This set is the
             ONLY grantor of the Acquisition_Deal_Actions custom permission; delete it and
             FeatureManagement.checkPermission simply returns false for everyone who used to
             hold it - no throw, no error, the buttons just stop working.

        THE CAPABILITY HALF NOW LIVES IN Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access (added 2026-08-10).
        The two sets are the two factors of one gate and must never be merged, in either
        direction. That file carries the mirror of this note.

        ASSIGNMENT IS AN ORG ACTION, NOT DEPLOYABLE METADATA, and this set is assigned DIRECTLY
        rather than through DPEG_Junior_Analyst_PSG - deliberately, because its population is
        narrower than any group's. Deploying it grants nobody anything.

        ⚠ AN ADMIN SMOKE TEST PROVES NOTHING ABOUT THIS GATE. A bare System Administrator does
        not hold the Acquisition_Deal_Actions custom permission either, unless separately
        assigned - Modify All Data is an OBJECT permission that confers no custom-permission
        grant. UPDATED - unlike the retired FLS mechanism, FeatureManagement.checkPermission
        never throws for an unassigned user, so there is no provisioning-gap risk of locking an
        administrator out; OpportunityActionPermissionService now checks Modify All Data AFTER
        the custom-permission read (the inverse of the retired ordering rule; see
        Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access's own header for the full note). Acceptance-test as a
        real deal-driver persona regardless.

        🔬 SPIKE (2026-08-12, PASSED) then PHASE 1 (2026-08-12) — READ IN ORDER AS HISTORY; the
        sections above this block already describe the LANDED, current mechanism (updated in the
        same pass that corrected this comment) - this SPIKE/PHASE 1 narrative is retained purely
        as the record of how the token was proven and rolled out, not as a still-pending caveat.
        The spike proved {!$Permission.CustomPermission.Acquisition_Deal_Actions} is honoured
        by the FlexiPage renderer at API 67.0 (granted-persona sees the button, non-granted
        does not), consumed then by exactly ONE FlexiPage criterion
        (Development_Feasibility_Review_Record_Page) and by NO Apex.

        PHASE 1 widened that to the remaining 18 acquisition-gated criteria across 6 more
        FlexiPages (see agent-output/design-requirements-custom-permissions.md) — the
        customPermissions grant below is the layer-5 AUTHORIZATION TOKEN for the whole
        Acquisitions deal-action gate's declarative half.
        UPDATED — a later developer pass swapped OpportunityActionPermissionService.cls off
        User.Deal_Driver__c entirely: it now calls
        FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Acquisition_Deal_Actions') as the sole
        server-side authorization check. The FLS-flag mechanism described above this note was
        RETIRED and governed nothing from that point on; it was left in place, superseded, as
        a rollback lever pending live UI verification.

        🔬 PHASE 2 STEP 1 (2026-08-12) — THE ROLLBACK LEVER IS SPENT, NOT HELD IN RESERVE.
        The custom-permission token was verified directly in the Lightning UI: on one NDA__c
        record sitting at the one remaining stage-criterion gap, the Advance Stage action was
        HIDDEN for a System Administrator holding no custom permission and VISIBLE for the real
        Junior Analyst persona holding Acquisition_Deal_Actions via this set. That confirms
        {!$Permission.CustomPermission.Acquisition_Deal_Actions} is honoured correctly by every
        one of the 24 acquisition/disposition-gated FlexiPage criteria and by the sole Apex
        consumer, with nothing still depending on the FLS flag in either direction. The
        User.Deal_Driver__c fieldPermissions grant has therefore been REMOVED from this file —
        it is not a rollback path any more, it is a deleted one. Reverting to flag-based
        visibility would require recreating the field, not just re-adding a grant here.
        User.Deal_Driver__c itself is NOT deleted by this step — that is a separate, later,
        destructive deploy.
    -->
    <description>Marks the &quot;Junior&quot; deal-driver. Grants the Acquisition_Deal_Actions custom permission (the live server-side authorization check). The retired User.Deal_Driver__c FLS grant was removed 2026-08-12 (Phase 2 Step 1) — see the class comment.</description>
    <customPermissions>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <name>Acquisition_Deal_Actions</name>
    </customPermissions>
    <flowAccesses>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <flow>Opportunity_Mark_Dead</flow>
    </flowAccesses>
    <hasActivationRequired>false</hasActivationRequired>
    <label>Acquisition Deal Driver</label>
</PermissionSet>
